{
  "dom_id": "step-453952",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Finding a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta?$",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta},$ we can calculate $\\sec\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\\right)} =\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 8} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 8}{8}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
